        ":question/worked-solution": "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\sin{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=z \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\sin{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=4 \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z}\\left(\\sin{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=x \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=f_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+f_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+f_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\ &= z \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,i+4 \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,j+x \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
